# DHCP settings for bootc PXE / UEFI HTTP boot

Generated by Ansible (role bootc_pxe) for PXE server **{{ bootc_pxe_server_ip }}**.
These are snippets: hand them to whoever runs your DHCP service. Your existing
DHCP server stays authoritative.

| Client (option 93 arch) | What it needs |
| --- | --- |
| UEFI x64 PXE (7 or 9) | next-server `{{ bootc_pxe_server_ip }}`, filename `{{ bootc_pxe_tftp_subdir }}/EFI/BOOT/{{ __bootc_pxe_efi_loader }}` (TFTP) |
| UEFI x64 HTTP Boot (16) | option 60 `HTTPClient`, filename `{{ bootc_pxe_http_url }}/EFI/BOOT/{{ __bootc_pxe_efi_loader }}` |
{% if bootc_pxe_bios_support %}
| Legacy BIOS (0) | next-server `{{ bootc_pxe_server_ip }}`, filename `{{ bootc_pxe_tftp_subdir }}/bios/i386-pc/core.0` |
{% endif %}

{% if bootc_pxe_secure_boot %}
`BOOTX64.EFI` is the Microsoft-signed **shim**. Secure Boot clients need it;
it then loads the vendor-signed `grubx64.efi` from the same directory.
{% endif %}

Files:
- `kea-dhcp4-client-classes.json`: Kea `client-classes` to merge into `Dhcp4`
- `isc-dhcpd.conf`: ISC dhcpd `class` blocks (ISC dhcpd is deprecated; RHEL 10 ships Kea)
- `infoblox.md`: the same values for Infoblox Grid Manager / WAPI
- `dnsmasq-proxy.conf`: proxy-DHCP alternative, for when you can't change DHCP

